# RSv1 ring SIGNATURE statement (anonymous "I am one of N enrolled members").
#
# Statement proved: "I am one of N enrolled members in the ring whose public
# root is R (a depth-d secret-direction Merkle tree under hirose_fixed_32), and
# I am binding the message m to this signature via Fiat-Shamir."  The signer's
# index is hidden (the per-level directions are secret), so the proof reveals
# nothing about which member produced it.  This is what makes the construction a
# ring signature rather than a named one: any of the N members could have made
# it, and the verifier cannot tell which.
#
# Message binding is a PROOF-LAYER concern, not a circuit concern.  The message m
# is NOT a wire here.  Instead the proof's Fiat-Shamir seed is derived from m
# (fs_seed = first 16 bytes of sha3_256(m)), so a proof produced for message m
# does not verify under a seed derived from any other message m'.  That binding
# is what makes this a signature (non-malleable over m) and not a bare
# membership proof.  See example_shipshape_ring_sig.c and the C-API equivalent
# example_ring_sig_v1_gf8.c.
#
# Privacy model: the secret key sk, the per-level directions dirs, and the
# sibling authentication path sib are ALL witness (private).  Only the ring root
# is public (INSTANCE).  The root is asserted internally by the construction
# (assertion-only entry, like the revocation non-membership construction), so
# there is NO ASSERT_EQUAL block here.
#
# Hash choice: ring_sig/v1[hirose_fixed_32] uses 32-byte nodes giving 128-bit
# collision resistance.  The secret key sk IS the signer's leaf record: skb is
# FROZEN at 32 (fixed_leaf_bytes = 32), and node_bytes = 32.  The construction
# hashes sk into the signer's leaf node internally.
#
# Tree shape: depth 5 (32-member ring).  ext (declaration order) =
#   sk(32) | dirs(5) | siblings(5 * 32 = 160) = 197 bytes.
# The root (32 bytes) is the INSTANCE and is NOT part of ext.  dirs[k] in {0,1}
# is bit k of the secret signer index, LSB first; the secret-dir mux enforces
# booleanity in-circuit.
#
# Scaling: the node width is fixed at 32 bytes by hirose_fixed_32, so to model a
# ring of depth D you change only two widths here: %sib becomes byte[D * 32] and
# %dirs becomes byte[D].  %sk and %root are depth-independent (both are one
# 32-byte record).  Proving cost grows linearly in D (one hirose inode hash per
# level).
.shipshape 1
field GF(2^8) irreducible 0x11B
stdlib crypto-v2

WITNESS  -> %sk   : byte[32]
WITNESS  -> %dirs : byte[5]
WITNESS  -> %sib  : byte[160]
INSTANCE -> %root : byte[32]

stdlib/crypto/ring_sig/v1[hirose_fixed_32](%sk, %dirs, %sib, %root)
